2026-09-28 CST / September 27 UTC — K1 assembler completion integrity (T05i)

RED: an actual sealed container successfully assembles the small
conservative-codegen-options.c output, prints OK a.o, then exits 17. The current
K1 assemble_batch accepts the object and can cache it despite the failed
container. Synthetic controls also expose duplicate/contradictory or unexpected
verdict acceptance; a reported success without an object raises FileNotFoundError
instead of recording a failed unit. Five assertions and five error subcases
fail in the initial 51-test survey-cache run.

Candidate kernel-assembly-verdict.patch:
  * Require zero container exit and exactly one OK/FAIL for every fresh unit.
  * Reject duplicate, contradictory, unexpected and missing records.
  * Require the actual object before hashing/publishing success.
  * Preserve assembler errors and process exit; bound recorded errors to 600
    characters. Do not populate successful cache entries on a failed batch.
  * Give the assembler cache a distinct protocol key so old unchecked entries
    cannot masquerade as newly checked success. Prepared checkpoints already
    include the implementation hash and invalidate when the implementation changes.

GREEN, ISOLATED CANDIDATE ONLY: all 51 survey-cache tests pass, candidate Ruff
passes, and the patch applies cleanly. Three actual sealed-container controls,
with cache disabled, produce:
  current implementation + actual exit 17: incorrectly assembled (RED)
  candidate + actual exit 17: assembler-refused, assemblerExitCode 17
  candidate + normal exit 0: assembled, assemblerExitCode 0
The successfully produced object hash is identical in the first and third cases.
The first native attempt had overlapping rw/ro mounts and failed setup; it is
retained as native-setup-error.json, not counted as acceptance evidence.

NOT INTEGRATED: the live kernel process 80898 already loaded substitute.py and
later reads that file again to fingerprint prepared checkpoints. Editing its
on-disk implementation mid-run would bind old in-memory execution to new source
bytes. Therefore the working production script remains byte-identical; only
a small isolated copy was tested, and this patch is queued for the next safe
run boundary. No live compiler, command projection, cache, object or process
was changed. This is not T05i closure or proof of the ongoing run's exit status.

At the safe boundary: apply the patch, run all K1 regressions and a real assembler
control, then use the hardened path for subsequent acceptance. Do not label old
receipts or the running 20260927T135325Z process as using this repair. No actual
nonzero assembler-container exit in that ongoing run has been demonstrated by
this separate failure-injection experiment.

